  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Near-normal with the weather pattern during upcoming week, with\n  dry conditions\n\n- Increasing winds Tuesday, especially across northwest Montana and\n  the higher terrain. \n\n- A few rain showers on Wednesday then warming trend into the\n  remainder of the week.\n\n- Confidence increasing for a warm, dry early October beyond the \n  7 day forecast. \n\nSatellite imagery shows a very large upper low over northern\nCalifornia which is pushing a lot moisture (in the form of thick\nmiddle clouds) into the Northern Rockies this afternoon. These \nclouds combined with the cold morning that we had has resulted in \ntemperatures climbing slower. High temperatures could be a few\ndegrees cooler than expected due the clouds today. Showers are \nseen on the radar extending from Lemhi County and points \neastwards. Also showers are poised to move over Idaho County.\nLater today, weak instability could result in a few weak\nthunderstorms from Lemhi County to southwest Montana.\nPrecipitation may struggle to make it to the ground in some places\ndue to dry air in place. \n\nA weak shortwave will move across the region on Monday. This will\nhelp the sun to come out and provide warming again. Also it will\nbring some breezy afternoon westerly winds, generally staying\nbelow 20 mph across northwest Montana. \n\nOn Tuesday, a weakening moisture plume that will have brought rain\nto southern British Columbia and northwest Washington State will\nbegin to impact northwest Montana. Increasing clouds, and westerly\nwinds will be felt. General west to southwest winds of 10 to 20\nmph with gusts to 30 mph can be expected. There's a 50% chance\nthat the US-2 corridor between Happy's Inn and Kalispell could\nexperience gusts exceeding 30 mph. Over the Divide in the Glacier\nRegion, there is a 80% chance for wind gusts exceeding 45 mph. For\nFlathead Lake the probabilities suggest that only a few places\ncould experience sustained 15 mph winds from Wild Horse Island to\nthe center of the lake, and also across southern Polson Bay.\nExpect choppy conditions. \n\nThe main moisture plume moves over northwest Montana later \nTuesday night into Wednesday morning. Expect periods of light rain\nmainly in the mountains. A few valley locations from Lowell, Idaho \nnorth and east to the Seeley/Swan Valley, to Glacier National Park\nhave a 40 to 50% chance of receiving measurable moisture in the \nmorning. \n\nA gradual warming trend will begin to end the week! \n\nConfidence is increasing for a warm, dry early October beyond the\n7 day forecast. The large scale weather pattern is being \ninfluenced by upstream tropical activity. With 2 hurricanes and 1 \ntropical cyclone in the central and east Pacific Ocean from Hawaii\nto Baja and a typhoon approaching the eastern periphery of Japan,\nit's been a difficult forecast for models. However, they're \nstarting to coalesce around higher chances for a potent ridge over\nthe northern Rockies into the first third of October. Ensemble \nmean temperature forecasts are coming in 15 degrees above seasonal\naverages at about 9-12 days out. Those are large anomalies for \nthat long of a lead time! Add in that the signal is coming from \nmultiple ensemble systems including both dynamical (physics based)\nand AI modeling systems, this provides above normal levels of \nconfidence for such a long lead time forecast. Looking back, the \nlast time this kind of warm ridge set up over the northwestern US \nin early October was october 9-11 and 14, 1991. Eureka, Montana \nhit 86 degrees, Grangeville, Idaho 87 degrees, and Butte 81 \ndegrees! The Center for Climate Prediction has issued forecasts \nfor the Northern Rockies region with a 70-80% chance of above \naverage temperatures and precipitation forecasts are leaning below\naverage for that time period as well.",
